WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.075
As an experienced developer, you don't want to start

2
00:00:05.075 --> 00:00:06.051
from zero.

3
00:00:06.051 --> 00:00:10.345
You already know how to build software — you

4
00:00:10.345 --> 00:00:15.517
don't need tutorials explaining what a variable is or

5
00:00:15.517 --> 00:00:17.176
how a loop works.

6
00:00:17.176 --> 00:00:23.324
At the same time, jumping straight into advanced cross-platform

7
00:00:23.324 --> 00:00:29.375
documentation can leave you without the structural context you

8
00:00:29.375 --> 00:00:29.863
need.

9
00:00:29.863 --> 00:00:34.644
The efficient path is to use the domain knowledge

10
00:00:34.644 --> 00:00:38.743
you already have — in web, iOS, or Android

11
00:00:38.743 --> 00:00:43.135
— to skip the basics and focus on translating

12
00:00:43.135 --> 00:00:46.258
what you know to a new platform.

13
00:00:46.258 --> 00:00:51.625
Heading into 2026, React Native has been rebuilt around

14
00:00:51.625 --> 00:00:57.480
a New Architecture: JSI replaces the old asynchronous bridge

15
00:00:57.480 --> 00:01:04.702
with direct, synchronous communication between JavaScript and native code.

16
00:01:04.702 --> 00:01:11.436
Compared to pure native development and web-to-mobile wrappers, React

17
00:01:11.436 --> 00:01:17.682
Native now offers near-native performance from a single codebase

18
00:01:17.682 --> 00:01:23.049
— the main investment is the learning curve, especially

19
00:01:23.049 --> 00:01:28.319
if you're coming from a different platform background.

20
00:01:28.319 --> 00:01:33.198
That's exactly what this course is built around: a

21
00:01:33.198 --> 00:01:39.639
structured curriculum with four distinct trails, each one designed

22
00:01:39.639 --> 00:01:44.519
to build on the expertise you already have instead

23
00:01:44.519 --> 00:01:45.983
of ignoring it.

24
00:01:45.983 --> 00:01:50.277
Let's look at what that means for each path.

25
00:01:50.277 --> 00:01:54.668
As a web engineer, your advantage is a strong

26
00:01:54.668 --> 00:01:59.157
grasp of the UI layer — components, hooks, and

27
00:01:59.157 --> 00:02:02.280
state logic carry over directly.

28
00:02:02.280 --> 00:02:06.965
What's different is the absence of the HTML DOM:

29
00:02:06.965 --> 00:02:10.575
there are no divs or spans on mobile.

30
00:02:10.575 --> 00:02:16.919
Instead, you'll work with strict mobile layout constraints, learn

31
00:02:16.919 --> 00:02:22.872
React Navigation, and bridge into native modules for hardware

32
00:02:22.872 --> 00:02:26.873
access like the camera and local storage.

33
00:02:26.873 --> 00:02:32.436
Web developers thrive here by shifting focus from browser

34
00:02:32.436 --> 00:02:38.193
syntax to how JavaScript talks to physical device hardware.

35
00:02:38.193 --> 00:02:42.878
For native developers coming from a solid iOS or

36
00:02:42.878 --> 00:02:49.123
Android background, your advantage is deep knowledge of platform

37
00:02:49.123 --> 00:02:55.760
lifecycles — you already understand app delegates, view controllers,

38
00:02:55.760 --> 00:03:00.444
or activities, which gives you a real head start

39
00:03:00.444 --> 00:03:05.714
in understanding how React Native talks to the device.

40
00:03:05.714 --> 00:03:10.398
What's new is trading your native UI framework —

41
00:03:10.398 --> 00:03:16.156
Swift or Jetpack Compose — for TypeScript and Flexbox-based

42
00:03:16.156 --> 00:03:16.839
layout.

43
00:03:16.839 --> 00:03:21.621
It's a different syntax, but it pays off quickly:

44
00:03:21.621 --> 00:03:24.353
one codebase, two platforms.

45
00:03:24.353 --> 00:03:29.526
There's a fourth path for developers who already have

46
00:03:29.526 --> 00:03:33.527
React Native experience: the Masterclass.

47
00:03:33.527 --> 00:03:39.968
It's built for engineers upgrading from legacy versions, replacing

48
00:03:39.968 --> 00:03:46.897
older workarounds with 2026's New Architecture patterns and synchronous

49
00:03:46.897 --> 00:03:48.458
threading model.

50
00:03:48.458 --> 00:03:53.045
Whichever path you take, you'll want a fast way

51
00:03:53.045 --> 00:03:55.387
to test ideas as you go.

52
00:03:55.387 --> 00:04:00.462
That's why every code example across all four trails

53
00:04:00.462 --> 00:04:05.634
runs immediately in your browser through Expo Snack —

54
00:04:05.634 --> 00:04:07.976
no local setup required.

55
00:04:07.976 --> 00:04:14.319
We deliberately leave environment configuration for later, so you

56
00:04:14.319 --> 00:04:18.809
can focus on learning the core concepts first.

57
00:04:18.809 --> 00:04:25.054
Despite very different starting points, all four trails converge

58
00:04:25.054 --> 00:04:30.715
on the same destination: fluency in TypeScript, mastery of

59
00:04:30.715 --> 00:04:35.301
Flexbox layout, and the ability to ship to both

60
00:04:35.301 --> 00:04:39.107
iOS and Android from a single codebase.

61
00:04:39.107 --> 00:04:44.475
Along the way, native developers will pick up CSS-style

62
00:04:44.475 --> 00:04:50.818
properties, and web developers will get comfortable with mobile's

63
00:04:50.818 --> 00:04:57.552
tighter memory and lifecycle constraints — different learning curves,

64
00:04:57.552 --> 00:04:59.211
same finish line.

65
00:04:59.211 --> 00:05:03.798
Your first step is simple: pick your trail, and

66
00:05:03.798 --> 00:05:08.970
start with the History & Architecture module for that

67
00:05:08.970 --> 00:05:09.458
path.

68
00:05:09.458 --> 00:05:14.532
Understanding why React Native looks the way it does

69
00:05:14.532 --> 00:05:19.900
in 2026 will make everything that follows click faster.
